\section{Measuring How Much Data Carries a Ranking}\label{sec:method}

\paragraph{Leaderboard and conclusions.}
A comparison dataset is a set of outcomes (model $a$ wins, model $b$ wins, tie) on $M$ models. We aggregate outcomes per model pair and fit Bradley--Terry scores $\theta\in\mathbb{R}^M$ by maximum likelihood, counting a tie as half a win for each side, as the Arena aggregation does~\cite{chiang2024} (ridge $10^{-4}$ on $\theta$ to fix the scale-free direction). A \emph{conclusion} is ``model $a$ is above model $b$'' with gap $\Delta_{ab}=\theta_a-\theta_b>0$. We study all $M(M-1)/2$ pairs of each dataset, so that the study covers the whole range of separations, and report separately the leaderboard conclusion ``top-1 above top-2'' and the published view (rank $=1+$ the number of models whose confidence interval lies above).

\paragraph{Statistical separation.}
$z=\Delta_{ab}/\mathrm{SE}(\Delta_{ab})$. We use the model-based (Fisher) standard error to define $z$ and the strata (z$\in[0,1),[1,2),[2,4),[4,8),[8,\infty)$), and a cluster bootstrap (500 resamples, clusters = battle, question or game) for the published confidence intervals and for the survival analysis; their ratio is the clustering design effect. All aggregate statements are limited to $z\in[1,8]$: below $1$ the pair is not separated, above $8$ both the bootstrap and the search extrapolate.

\paragraph{Alterations and their cost.}
An \emph{alteration} changes one comparison and costs 1. \emph{Deletion} removes a comparison; it is not an action anyone can take on crowd votes, and measures how much of the evidence carries the conclusion~\cite{broderick2020}. \emph{Outcome flip} changes the outcome of an existing comparison (tie to win, loss to win; a win turned into a loss costs 1, with a sensitivity check at cost 2): it is the effect of a labelling error or of an adversary changing labels. Free-choice \emph{insertion} of new votes is a lower bound on the attacker's cost, because on Arena inserted votes go through the sampler~\cite{min2025}; it is not part of the core study.

\paragraph{Fragility $k^\*$.}
$k^\*(a\succ b)$ is the smallest number of alterations after which the exact refit has $\theta_a-\theta_b\le 0$. It is found by search and is a \emph{verified upper bound}: the search proposes alterations by first-order influence (the effect of one alteration on the gap, $H^{-1}$-propagated as in influence functions~\cite{koh2017}), fills the budget with the most effective ones, refits exactly, and re-linearises at the refit (iterated re-linearisation); a Frank--Wolfe pass on the continuous relaxation is a second proposer. The number of alterations is found by bisection on the budget. Let $g_{\max}$ be the largest first-order effect of a single alteration on the gap; $k_{\mathrm{lin}}$ is the first-order estimate (the smallest $k$ whose $k$ largest effects sum to the gap) without refit.

\paragraph{Scale across datasets.}
For a fixed conclusion, the statistical separation is $z\propto\Delta\sqrt N$ and the largest influence decreases with $N$, so the raw share $k^\*/N$ is not comparable between datasets of different size; theory for top-$K$ ranking gives the same $1/\sqrt N$ rate for the estimation error~\cite{chen2019}. We therefore express fragility in standard-error units, $F=k^\*g_{\max}/\mathrm{SE}$, and the dimensionless ratio $Q=F/z=k^\*g_{\max}/\Delta$. $Q$ equals~1 in the first-order regime if all single-alteration effects were equal to $g_{\max}$; exact refit and the shape of the influence distribution move it, depending on the family and the comparison graph. $Q$ is therefore read only against its own reference (below).

